ses the potential for LLMs to complement single-cell foundation models and drive improvements in single-cell analysis.",include_decision_unstable,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,unclear,unclear,no,no,,,,,"The abstract describes scMPT as a fusion of scGPT and LLM representations for downstream single-cell tasks, with no natural-language text input/output or generative text-bio bridge, fitting the application wrapper exclusion. | The abstract presents scMPT as a fusion of scGPT and LLM representations, focusing on downstream task performance without describing a generative architecture or foundation-model characteristics; it is an application wrapper.","The abstract describes scMPT as a fusion of scGPT and LLM representations for downstream single-cell tasks, with no natural-language text input/output or generative text-bio bridge, fitting the application wrapper exclusion.","The abstract presents scMPT as a fusion of scGPT and LLM representations, focusing on downstream task performance without describing a generative architecture or foundation-model characteristics; it is an application wrapper.",,Towards Applying Large Language Models to Complement Single-Cell Foundation Models,"Single-cell foundation models such as scGPT represent a significant advancement in single-cell omics, with an ability to achieve state-of-the-art performance on various downstream biological tasks. However, these models are inherently limited in that a vast amount of information in biology exists as text, which they are unable to leverage. There have therefore been several recent works that propose the use of LLMs as an alternative to single-cell foundation models, achieving competitive results. However, there is little understanding of what factors drive this performance, along with a strong focus on using LLMs as an alternative, rather than complementary approach to single-cell foundation models. In this study, we therefore investigate what biological insights contribute toward the performance of LLMs when applied to single-cell data, and introduce scMPT; a model which leverages synergies between scGPT, and single-cell representations from LLMs that capture these insights. scMPT demonstrates stronger, more consistent performance than either of its component models, which frequently have large performance gaps between each other across datasets. We also experiment with alternate fusion methods, demonstrating the potential of combining specialized reasoning models with scGPT to improve performance. This study ultimately showcases the potential for LLMs to complement single-cell foundation models and drive improvements in single-cell analysis.",include_decision_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC3_not_generative,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,no,unclear,,,,,"The paper introduces scMPT, a new model that fuses a single‑cell foundation model with LLM‑derived representations, establishing a substantive text‑bio multimodal bridge. | The abstract introduces scMPT, a model that fuses scGPT and LLM representations, but it does not mention any decoder, autoregressive, or other generative mechanism. It references foundation models (scGPT, LLMs) but does not provide evidence that scMPT itself is a broadly pretrained foundation model.","The paper introduces scMPT, a new model that fuses a single‑cell foundation model with LLM‑derived representations, establishing a substantive text‑bio multimodal bridge.","The abstract introduces scMPT, a model that fuses scGPT and LLM representations, but it does not mention any decoder, autoregressive, or other generative mechanism. It references foundation models (scGPT, LLMs) but does not provide evidence that scMPT itself is a broadly pretrained foundation model.",,Towards Applying Large Language Models to Complement Single-Cell Foundation Models,"Single-cell foundation models such as scGPT represent a significant advancement in single-cell omics, with an ability to achieve state-of-the-art performance on various downstream biological tasks. However, these models are inherently limited in that a vast amount of information in biology exists as text, which they are unable to leverage. There have therefore been several recent works that propose the use of LLMs as an alternative to single-cell foundation models, achieving competitive results. However, there is little understanding of what factors drive this performance, along with a strong focus on using LLMs as an alternative, rather than complementary approach to single-cell foundation models. In this study, we therefore investigate what biological insights contribute toward the performance of LLMs when applied to single-cell data, and introduce scMPT; a model which leverages synergies between scGPT, and single-cell representations from LLMs that capture these insights. scMPT demonstrates stronger, more consistent performance than either of its component models, which frequently have large performance gaps between each other across datasets. We also experiment with alternate fusion methods, demonstrating the potential of combining specialized reasoning models with scGPT to improve performance. This study ultimately showcases the potential for LLMs to complement single-cell foundation models and drive improvements in single-cell analysis.",include_decision_unstable,UNCERTAIN,,text_component_unclear,primary_model_paper,unclear,yes,no,yes,none,"leverages synergies between scGPT, and single-cell representations from LLMs that capture these insights",none,thin_abstract_boundary,"The abstract presents scMPT, a new model that fuses scGPT with LLM‑derived representations, indicating a bridge between text‑trained models and single‑cell data, but it does not explicitly describe natural‑language text input or generation, leaving the text component status uncertain.","The study presents scMPT, a new multimodal model combining single‑cell omics with LLM representations, indicating a primary model with a clear biological modality. The abstract references textual biological information and a synergy between LLMs and omics, suggesting a text‑bio bridge, but it does not specify natural‑language input, making the text component uncertain.","The study introduces scMPT, a fused model that combines scGPT with LLM-derived representations; the abstract mentions no decoder, autoregressive or other generative mechanism.","The abstract presents scMPT, a new model that fuses scGPT with LLM‑derived representations, indicating a bridge between text‑trained models and single‑cell data, but it does not explicitly describe natural‑language text input or generation, leaving the text component status uncertain."
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement_current_3ds_2gpt/cross_model_decisions.csv:579:                We introduce Cell2Sentence (C2S), a novel method to directly adapt large language models to a biological context, specifically single-cell transcriptomics. By transforming gene expression data into “cell sentences,” C2S bridges the gap between natural language processing and biology. We demonstrate cell sentences enable the fine-tuning of language models for diverse tasks in biology, including cell generation, complex cell-type annotation, and direct data-driven text generation. Our experiments reveal that GPT-2, when fine-tuned with C2S, can generate biologically valid cells based on cell type inputs, and accurately predict cell types from cell sentences. This illustrates that language models, through C2S fine-tuning, can acquire a significant understanding of single-cell biology while maintaining robust text generation capabilities. C2S offers a flexible, accessible framework to integrate natural language processing with transcriptomics, utilizing existing models and libraries for a wide range of biological applications.",stable_include,Cell2Sentence: teaching large language models the language of biology,"Abstract
