d the development of “virtual cells.”",benchmark_boundary_case,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract presents a new LLM (C2S-Scale) that is trained on both transcriptomic data and biological text, with fine-tuning for natural language interpretation and biological reasoning, clearly satisfying all inclusion criteria. | The abstract describes training a large-scale LLM on transcriptomic and textual data, with explicit mention of generative capabilities and scaling to 27B parameters, indicating a primary generative foundation model for single-cell analysis.","The abstract presents a new LLM (C2S-Scale) that is trained on both transcriptomic data and biological text, with fine-tuning for natural language interpretation and biological reasoning, clearly satisfying all inclusion criteria.","The abstract describes training a large-scale LLM on transcriptomic and textual data, with explicit mention of generative capabilities and scaling to 27B parameters, indicating a primary generative foundation model for single-cell analysis.",,Scaling Large Language Models for Next-Generation Single-Cell Analysis.,"A bstract Single-cell RNA sequencing has transformed our understanding of cellular diversity, yet current single-cell foundation models (scFMs) remain limited in their scalability, flexibility across diverse tasks, and ability to natively integrate textual information. In this work, we build upon the Cell2Sentence (C2S) framework, which represents scRNA-seq profiles as textual “cell sentences,” to train Large Language Models (LLMs) on a corpus comprising over one billion tokens of transcriptomic data, biological text, and metadata. Scaling the model to 27 billion parameters yields consistent improvements in predictive and generative capabilities and supports advanced downstream tasks that require synthesis of information across multi-cellular contexts. Targeted fine-tuning with modern reinforcement learning techniques produces strong performance in perturbation response prediction, natural language interpretation, and complex biological reasoning. This predictive strength enabled a dual-context virtual screen that nominated the kinase inhibitor silmitasertib (CX-4945) as a candidate for context-selective upregulation of antigen presentation. Experimental assessment in human cell models unseen during training supported this prediction, demonstrating that C2S-Scale can effectively guide the discovery of context-conditioned biology. C2S-Scale unifies transcriptomic and textual data at unprecedented scales, surpassing both specialized single-cell models and general-purpose LLMs to provide a platform for next-generation single-cell analysis and the development of “virtual cells.”",benchmark_boundary_case,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract introduces a new 27‑billion‑parameter model (C2S‑Scale) that jointly trains on scRNA‑seq data and natural‑language text, indicating a primary model with both biological modality and substantive text‑bio integration. | The abstract reports a 27‑billion‑parameter LLM trained on a billion‑token corpus of scRNA‑seq, biological text, and metadata, and explicitly mentions generative capabilities. This positions the work as a primary generative text‑bio foundation model rather than a wrapper or benchmark.","The abstract introduces a new 27‑billion‑parameter model (C2S‑Scale) that jointly trains on scRNA‑seq data and natural‑language text, indicating a primary model with both biological modality and substantive text‑bio integration.","The abstract reports a 27‑billion‑parameter LLM trained on a billion‑token corpus of scRNA‑seq, biological text, and metadata, and explicitly mentions generative capabilities. This positions the work as a primary generative text‑bio foundation model rather than a wrapper or benchmark.",,Scaling Large Language Models for Next-Generation Single-Cell Analysis.,"A bstract Single-cell RNA sequencing has transformed our understanding of cellular diversity, yet current single-cell foundation models (scFMs) remain limited in their scalability, flexibility across diverse tasks, and ability to natively integrate textual information. In this work, we build upon the Cell2Sentence (C2S) framework, which represents scRNA-seq profiles as textual “cell sentences,” to train Large Language Models (LLMs) on a corpus comprising over one billion tokens of transcriptomic data, biological text, and metadata. Scaling the model to 27 billion parameters yields consistent improvements in predictive and generative capabilities and supports advanced downstream tasks that require synthesis of information across multi-cellular contexts. Targeted fine-tuning with modern reinforcement learning techniques produces strong performance in perturbation response prediction, natural language interpretation, and complex biological reasoning. This predictive strength enabled a dual-context virtual screen that nominated the kinase inhibitor silmitasertib (CX-4945) as a candidate for context-selective upregulation of antigen presentation. Experimental assessment in human cell models unseen during training supported this prediction, demonstrating that C2S-Scale can effectively guide the discovery of context-conditioned biology. C2S-Scale unifies transcriptomic and textual data at unprecedented scales, surpassing both specialized single-cell models and general-purpose LLMs to provide a platform for next-generation single-cell analysis and the development of “virtual cells.”",benchmark_boundary_case,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract presents C2S-Scale, a new LLM trained on combined transcriptomic and textual corpora, establishing a primary model that integrates a biological modality with a substantive natural‑language component, forming a clear text‑bio bridge. | The abstract introduces a 27‑billion‑parameter LLM trained on transcriptomic and textual data, explicitly noting generative capabilities, indicating a primary generative foundation model.","The abstract presents C2S-Scale, a new LLM trained on combined transcriptomic and textual corpora, establishing a primary model that integrates a biological modality with a substantive natural‑language component, forming a clear text‑bio bridge.","The abstract introduces a 27‑billion‑parameter LLM trained on transcriptomic and textual data, explicitly noting generative capabilities, indicating a primary generative foundation model.",
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement/cross_model_decisions.csv:265:1272,Towards Applying Large Language Models to Complement Single-Cell Foundation Models,"Single-cell foundation models such as scGPT represent a significant advancement in single-cell omics, with an ability to achieve state-of-the-art performance on various downstream biological tasks. However, these models are inherently limited in that a vast amount of information in biology exists as text, which they are unable to leverage. There have therefore been several recent works that propose the use of LLMs as an alternative to single-cell foundation models, achieving competitive results. However, there is little understanding of what factors drive this performance, along with a strong focus on using LLMs as an alternative, rather than complementary approach to single-cell foundation models. In this study, we therefore investigate what biological insights contribute toward the performance of LLMs when applied to single-cell data, and introduce scMPT; a model which leverages synergies between scGPT, and single-cell representations from LLMs that capture these insights. scMPT demonstrates stronger, more consistent performance than either of its component models, which frequently have large performance gaps between each other across datasets. We also experiment with alternate fusion methods, demonstrating the potential of combining specialized reasoning models with scGPT to improve performance. This study ultimately showcases the potential for LLMs to complement single-cell foundation models and drive improvements in single-cell analysis.",include_decision_unstable,Towards Applying Large Language Models to Complement Single-Cell Foundation Models,"Single-cell foundation models such as scGPT represent a significant advancement in single-cell omics, with an ability to achieve state-of-the-art performance on various downstream biological tasks. However, these models are inherently limited in that a vast amount of information in biology exists as text, which they are unable to leverage. There have therefore been several recent works that propose the use of LLMs as an alternative to single-cell foundation models, achieving competitive results. However, there is little understanding of what factors drive this performance, along with a strong focus on using LLMs as an alternative, rather than complementary approach to single-cell foundation models. In this study, we therefore investigate what biological insights contribute toward the performance of LLMs when applied to single-cell data, and introduce scMPT; a model which leverages synergies between scGPT, and single-cell representations from LLMs that capture these insights. scMPT demonstrates stronger, more consistent performance than either of its component models, which frequently have large performance gaps between each other across datasets. We also experiment with alternate fusion methods, demonstrating the potential of combining specialized reasoning models with scGPT to improve performance. This study ultimately showcases the potential for LLMs to complement single-cell foundation models and drive improvements in single-cell analysis.",include_decision_unstable,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,no,no,no,yes,none,none,none,wrapper_boundary,"scMPT fuses scGPT and LLM representations without generating text or conditioning on natural-language input; it applies existing LLMs as complementary, fitting application_wrapper exclusion.","The abstract introduces scMPT, which combines scGPT with LLM representations of single-cell data, but no natural-language text input, output, or conditioning is described; thus the text component criterion is not met.","scMPT fuses scGPT and LLM representations for single-cell analysis, not a new generative model; it is an application wrapper around existing foundation models.","scMPT fuses scGPT and LLM representations without generating text or conditioning on natural-language input; it applies existing LLMs as complementary, fitting application_wrapper exclusion.",Towards Applying Large Language Models to Complement Single-Cell Foundation Models,"Single-cell foundation models such as scGPT represent a significant advancement in single-cell omics, with an ability to achieve state-of-the-art performance on various downstream biological tasks. However, these models are inherently limited in that a vast amount of information in biology exists as text, which they are unable to leverage. There have therefore been several recent works that propose the use of LLMs as an alternative to single-cell foundation models, achieving competitive results. However, there is little understanding of what factors drive this performance, along with a strong focus on using LLMs as an alternative, rather than complementary approach to single-cell foundation models. In this study, we therefore investigate what biological insights contribute toward the performance of LLMs when applied to single-cell data, and introduce scMPT; a model which leverages synergies between scGPT, and single-cell representations from LLMs that capture these insights. scMPT demonstrates stronger, more consistent performance than either of its component models, which frequently have large performance gaps between each other across datasets. We also experiment with alternate fusion methods, demonstrating the potential of combining specialized reasoning models with scGPT to improve performance. This study ultimately showcases the potential for LLMs to complement single-cell foundation models and drive improvements in single-cell analysis.",include_decision_unstable,EXCLUDE,application_wrapper,,application_wrapper,no,no,no,unclear,,,,,"The paper introduces scMPT, a fusion model that uses external LLM representations as side information to complement scGPT for single-cell analysis, fitting the application_wrapper exclusion. | The abstract describes scMPT as a fusion of scGPT and LLM representations for single-cell analysis, with no explicit generative mechanism; it is an application wrapper rather than a primary generative model.","The paper introduces scMPT, a fusion model that uses external LLM representations as side information to complement scGPT for single-cell analysis, fitting the application_wrapper exclusion.","The abstract describes scMPT as a fusion of scGPT and LLM representations for single-cell analysis, with no explicit generative mechanism; it is an application wrapper rather than a primary generative model.",,Towards Applying Large Language Models to Complement Single-Cell Foundation Models,"Single-cell foundation models such as scGPT represent a significant advancement in single-cell omics, with an ability to achieve state-of-the-art performance on various downstream biological tasks. However, these models are inherently limited in that a vast amount of information in biology exists as text, which they are unable to leverage. There have therefore been several recent works that propose the use of LLMs as an alternative to single-cell foundation models, achieving competitive results. However, there is little understanding of what factors drive this performance, along with a strong focus on using LLMs as an alternative, rather than complementary approach to single-cell foundation models. In this study, we therefore investigate what biological insights contribute toward the performance of LLMs when applied to single-cell data, and introduce scMPT; a model which leverages synergies between scGPT, and single-cell representations from LLMs that capture these insights. scMPT demonstrates stronger, more consistent performance than either of its component models, which frequently have large performance gaps between each other across datasets. We also experiment with alternate fusion methods, demonstrating the potential of combining specialized reasoning models with scGPT to improve performance. This study ultimately showcases the potential for LLMs to complement single-cell foundation models and drive improvements in single-cell analysis.",include_decision_unstable,EXCLUDE,EC3_not_generative,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,no,unclear,,,,,"The paper introduces scMPT, a new model that fuses a single‑cell foundation model with LLM‑derived representations, establishing a substantive text‑bio multimodal bridge. | The abstract introduces scMPT, a model that fuses scGPT and LLM representations, but it does not mention any decoder, autoregressive, or other generative mechanism. It references foundation models (scGPT, LLMs) but does not provide evidence that scMPT itself is a broadly pretrained foundation model.","The paper introduces scMPT, a new model that fuses a single‑cell foundation model with LLM‑derived representations, establishing a substantive text‑bio multimodal bridge.","The abstract introduces scMPT, a model that fuses scGPT and LLM representations, but it does not mention any decoder, autoregressive, or other generative mechanism. It references foundation models (scGPT, LLMs) but does not provide evidence that scMPT itself is a broadly pretrained foundation model.",,Towards Applying Large Language Models to Complement Single-Cell Foundation Models,"Single-cell foundation models such as scGPT represent a significant advancement in single-cell omics, with an ability to achieve state-of-the-art performance on various downstream biological tasks. However, these models are inherently limited in that a vast amount of information in biology exists as text, which they are unable to leverage. There have therefore been several recent works that propose the use of LLMs as an alternative to single-cell foundation models, achieving competitive results. However, there is little understanding of what factors drive this performance, along with a strong focus on using LLMs as an alternative, rather than complementary approach to single-cell foundation models. In this study, we therefore investigate what biological insights contribute toward the performance of LLMs when applied to single-cell data, and introduce scMPT; a model which leverages synergies between scGPT, and single-cell representations from LLMs that capture these insights. scMPT demonstrates stronger, more consistent performance than either of its component models, which frequently have large performance gaps between each other across datasets. We also experiment with alternate fusion methods, demonstrating the potential of combining specialized reasoning models with scGPT to improve performance. This study ultimately showcases the potential for LLMs to complement single-cell foundation models and drive improvements in single-cell analysis.",include_decision_unstable,UNCERTAIN,,text_component_unclear,primary_model_paper,unclear,yes,no,yes,none,"leverages synergies between scGPT, and single-cell representations from LLMs that capture these insights",none,thin_abstract_boundary,"The abstract presents scMPT, a new model that fuses scGPT with LLM‑derived representations, indicating a bridge between text‑trained models and single‑cell data, but it does not explicitly describe natural‑language text input or generation, leaving the text component status uncertain.","The study presents scMPT, a new multimodal model combining single‑cell omics with LLM representations, indicating a primary model with a clear biological modality. The abstract references textual biological information and a synergy between LLMs and omics, suggesting a text‑bio bridge, but it does not specify natural‑language input, making the text component uncertain.","The study introduces scMPT, a fused model that combines scGPT with LLM-derived representations; the abstract mentions no decoder, autoregressive or other generative mechanism.","The abstract presents scMPT, a new model that fuses scGPT with LLM‑derived representations, indicating a bridge between text‑trained models and single‑cell data, but it does not explicitly describe natural‑language text input or generation, leaving the text component status uncertain."
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement/cross_model_decisions.csv:498:                We introduce Cell2Sentence (C2S), a novel method to directly adapt large language models to a biological context, specifically single-cell transcriptomics. By transforming gene expression data into “cell sentences,” C2S bridges the gap between natural language processing and biology. We demonstrate cell sentences enable the fine-tuning of language models for diverse tasks in biology, including cell generation, complex cell-type annotation, and direct data-driven text generation. Our experiments reveal that GPT-2, when fine-tuned with C2S, can generate biologically valid cells based on cell type inputs, and accurately predict cell types from cell sentences. This illustrates that language models, through C2S fine-tuning, can acquire a significant understanding of single-cell biology while maintaining robust text generation capabilities. C2S offers a flexible, accessible framework to integrate natural language processing with transcriptomics, utilizing existing models and libraries for a wide range of biological applications.",stable_include,Cell2Sentence: teaching large language models the language of biology,"Abstract
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement/cross_model_decisions.csv:499:                We introduce Cell2Sentence (C2S), a novel method to directly adapt large language models to a biological context, specifically single-cell transcriptomics. By transforming gene expression data into “cell sentences,” C2S bridges the gap between natural language processing and biology. We demonstrate cell sentences enable the fine-tuning of language models for diverse tasks in biology, including cell generation, complex cell-type annotation, and direct data-driven text generation. Our experiments reveal that GPT-2, when fine-tuned with C2S, can generate biologically valid cells based on cell type inputs, and accurately predict cell types from cell sentences. This illustrates that language models, through C2S fine-tuning, can acquire a significant understanding of single-cell biology while maintaining robust text generation capabilities. C2S offers a flexible, accessible framework to integrate natural language processing with transcriptomics, utilizing existing models and libraries for a wide range of biological applications.",stable_include,UNCERTAIN,,text_component_unclear,primary_model_paper,unclear,unclear,yes,yes,direct data-driven text generation,bridges the gap between natural language processing and biology,can generate biologically valid cells,thin_abstract_boundary,"Paper presents Cell2Sentence fine-tuning GPT-2 on cell sentences. Text component and text-bio bridge are unclear because 'cell sentences' are biological token sequences, not natural-language text; text generation mention is ambiguous. Generative model is clear from cell generation and GPT-2 fine-tuning.","The abstract describes a method that adapts LLMs to single-cell transcriptomics via 'cell sentences,' but it is ambiguous whether these sentences constitute natural-language text or biological tokens. The text component and text-bio bridge are unclear due to lack of explicit natural-language input/output or conditioning, leading to a thin_abstract_boundary.","The abstract describes fine-tuning GPT-2, a generative LLM, for cell generation and text generation tasks in single-cell biology, clearly meeting generative and foundation-model criteria.","Paper presents Cell2Sentence fine-tuning GPT-2 on cell sentences. Text component and text-bio bridge are unclear because 'cell sentences' are biological token sequences, not natural-language text; text generation mention is ambiguous. Generative model is clear from cell generation and GPT-2 fine-tuning.",Cell2Sentence: teaching large language models the language of biology,"Abstract
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement/cross_model_decisions.csv:500:                We introduce Cell2Sentence (C2S), a novel method to directly adapt large language models to a biological context, specifically single-cell transcriptomics. By transforming gene expression data into “cell sentences,” C2S bridges the gap between natural language processing and biology. We demonstrate cell sentences enable the fine-tuning of language models for diverse tasks in biology, including cell generation, complex cell-type annotation, and direct data-driven text generation. Our experiments reveal that GPT-2, when fine-tuned with C2S, can generate biologically valid cells based on cell type inputs, and accurately predict cell types from cell sentences. This illustrates that language models, through C2S fine-tuning, can acquire a significant understanding of single-cell biology while maintaining robust text generation capabilities. C2S offers a flexible, accessible framework to integrate natural language processing with transcriptomics, utilizing existing models and libraries for a wide range of biological applications.",stable_include,EXCLUDE,EC2_no_text_component,,primary_model_paper,no,no,yes,yes,direct data-driven text generation,transforming gene expression data into 'cell sentences',"cell generation, direct data-driven text generation",text_component_boundary,"The abstract presents Cell2Sentence as a method to adapt LLMs to single-cell transcriptomics using cell sentences (biological tokens), not natural-language text. While the model is generative and uses GPT-2, the text component is not clearly natural language, so text_component_present=no and text_bio_bridge_present=no, leading to exclusion EC2_no_text_component. The paper is a primary model paper, not a wrapper, because it introduces a fine-tuning method.","The abstract describes transforming gene expression data into 'cell sentences,' which are biological token sequences, not natural-language text. Therefore, the candidate model lacks a substantive text/language component.","The paper fine-tunes an existing generative model (GPT-2) for biological tasks, making it an application wrapper rather than a new foundational model.","The abstract presents Cell2Sentence as a method to adapt LLMs to single-cell transcriptomics using cell sentences (biological tokens), not natural-language text. While the model is generative and uses GPT-2, the text component is not clearly natural language, so text_component_present=no and text_bio_bridge_present=no, leading to exclusion EC2_no_text_component. The paper is a primary model paper, not a wrapper, because it introduces a fine-tuning method.",Cell2Sentence: teaching large language models the language of biology,"Abstract
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement/cross_model_decisions.csv:501:                We introduce Cell2Sentence (C2S), a novel method to directly adapt large language models to a biological context, specifically single-cell transcriptomics. By transforming gene expression data into “cell sentences,” C2S bridges the gap between natural language processing and biology. We demonstrate cell sentences enable the fine-tuning of language models for diverse tasks in biology, including cell generation, complex cell-type annotation, and direct data-driven text generation. Our experiments reveal that GPT-2, when fine-tuned with C2S, can generate biologically valid cells based on cell type inputs, and accurately predict cell types from cell sentences. This illustrates that language models, through C2S fine-tuning, can acquire a significant understanding of single-cell biology while maintaining robust text generation capabilities. C2S offers a flexible, accessible framework to integrate natural language processing with transcriptomics, utilizing existing models and libraries for a wide range of biological applications.",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The paper introduces Cell2Sentence, a novel method that converts single‑cell transcriptomics into natural‑language sentences and fine‑tunes GPT‑2 to generate biologically valid cells, demonstrating a clear text‑bio multimodal model. | The abstract introduces Cell2Sentence, fine‑tuning GPT‑2 to generate cells and text, showing a generative decoder architecture built on a pre‑trained large language model.","The paper introduces Cell2Sentence, a novel method that converts single‑cell transcriptomics into natural‑language sentences and fine‑tunes GPT‑2 to generate biologically valid cells, demonstrating a clear text‑bio multimodal model.","The abstract introduces Cell2Sentence, fine‑tuning GPT‑2 to generate cells and text, showing a generative decoder architecture built on a pre‑trained large language model.",,Cell2Sentence: teaching large language models the language of biology,"Abstract
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement/cross_model_decisions.csv:502:                We introduce Cell2Sentence (C2S), a novel method to directly adapt large language models to a biological context, specifically single-cell transcriptomics. By transforming gene expression data into “cell sentences,” C2S bridges the gap between natural language processing and biology. We demonstrate cell sentences enable the fine-tuning of language models for diverse tasks in biology, including cell generation, complex cell-type annotation, and direct data-driven text generation. Our experiments reveal that GPT-2, when fine-tuned with C2S, can generate biologically valid cells based on cell type inputs, and accurately predict cell types from cell sentences. This illustrates that language models, through C2S fine-tuning, can acquire a significant understanding of single-cell biology while maintaining robust text generation capabilities. C2S offers a flexible, accessible framework to integrate natural language processing with transcriptomics, utilizing existing models and libraries for a wide range of biological applications.",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract introduces Cell2Sentence, a new method that converts single‑cell transcriptomics into textual sentences and fine‑tunes GPT‑2, demonstrating generation of cells from text inputs and prediction of cell types from sentences; this constitutes a primary multimodal generative model linking natural language and biology. | The abstract reports fine‑tuning GPT‑2 (a decoder LLM) to generate cells and text, confirming a generative architecture; it also leverages a pre‑trained large language model, indicating foundation‑model properties.","The abstract introduces Cell2Sentence, a new method that converts single‑cell transcriptomics into textual sentences and fine‑tunes GPT‑2, demonstrating generation of cells from text inputs and prediction of cell types from sentences; this constitutes a primary multimodal generative model linking natural language and biology.","The abstract reports fine‑tuning GPT‑2 (a decoder LLM) to generate cells and text, confirming a generative architecture; it also leverages a pre‑trained large language model, indicating foundation‑model properties.",
./analysis/cross_agent_screening_agreement/cross_model_decisions.csv:593:89,Scaling Large Language Models for Next-Generation Single-Cell Analysis.,"A bstract Single-cell RNA sequencing has transformed our understanding of cellular diversity, yet current single-cell foundation models (scFMs) remain limited in their scalability, flexibility across diverse tasks, and ability to natively integrate textual information. In this work, we build upon the Cell2Sentence (C2S) framework, which represents scRNA-seq profiles as textual “cell sentences,” to train Large Language Models (LLMs) on a corpus comprising over one billion tokens of transcriptomic data, biological text, and metadata. Scaling the model to 27 billion parameters yields consistent improvements in predictive and generative capabilities and supports advanced downstream tasks that require synthesis of information across multi-cellular contexts. Targeted fine-tuning with modern reinforcement learning techniques produces strong performance in perturbation response prediction, natural language interpretation, and complex biological reasoning. This predictive strength enabled a dual-context virtual screen that nominated the kinase inhibitor silmitasertib (CX-4945) as a candidate for context-selective upregulation of antigen presentation. Experimental assessment in human cell models unseen during training supported this prediction, demonstrating that C2S-Scale can effectively guide the discovery of context-conditioned biology. C2S-Scale unifies transcriptomic and textual data at unprecedented scales, surpassing both specialized single-cell models and general-purpose LLMs to provide a platform for next-generation single-cell analysis and the development of “virtual cells.”",stable_include,Scaling Large Language Models for Next-Generation Single-Cell Analysis.,"A bstract Single-cell RNA sequencing has transformed our understanding of cellular diversity, yet current single-cell foundation models (scFMs) remain limited in their scalability, flexibility across diverse tasks, and ability to natively integrate textual information. In this work, we build upon the Cell2Sentence (C2S) framework, which represents scRNA-seq profiles as textual “cell sentences,” to train Large Language Models (LLMs) on a corpus comprising over one billion tokens of transcriptomic data, biological text, and metadata. Scaling the model to 27 billion parameters yields consistent improvements in predictive and generative capabilities and supports advanced downstream tasks that require synthesis of information across multi-cellular contexts. Targeted fine-tuning with modern reinforcement learning techniques produces strong performance in perturbation response prediction, natural language interpretation, and complex biological reasoning. This predictive strength enabled a dual-context virtual screen that nominated the kinase inhibitor silmitasertib (CX-4945) as a candidate for context-selective upregulation of antigen presentation. Experimental assessment in human cell models unseen during training supported this prediction, demonstrating that C2S-Scale can effectively guide the discovery of context-conditioned biology. C2S-Scale unifies transcriptomic and textual data at unprecedented scales, surpassing both specialized single-cell models and general-purpose LLMs to provide a platform for next-generation single-cell analysis and the development of “virtual cells.”",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract presents a new LLM (C2S-Scale) trained on both transcriptomic and textual data, with fine-tuning for natural language interpretation and biological reasoning, clearly satisfying text component and text-bio bridge criteria. | The abstract describes training a 27-billion-parameter LLM on transcriptomic and textual data, explicitly mentioning generative capabilities and broad applicability across tasks, indicating a primary generative foundational model.","The abstract presents a new LLM (C2S-Scale) trained on both transcriptomic and textual data, with fine-tuning for natural language interpretation and biological reasoning, clearly satisfying text component and text-bio bridge criteria.","The abstract describes training a 27-billion-parameter LLM on transcriptomic and textual data, explicitly mentioning generative capabilities and broad applicability across tasks, indicating a primary generative foundational model.",,Scaling Large Language Models for Next-Generation Single-Cell Analysis.,"A bstract Single-cell RNA sequencing has transformed our understanding of cellular diversity, yet current single-cell foundation models (scFMs) remain limited in their scalability, flexibility across diverse tasks, and ability to natively integrate textual information. In this work, we build upon the Cell2Sentence (C2S) framework, which represents scRNA-seq profiles as textual “cell sentences,” to train Large Language Models (LLMs) on a corpus comprising over one billion tokens of transcriptomic data, biological text, and metadata. Scaling the model to 27 billion parameters yields consistent improvements in predictive and generative capabilities and supports advanced downstream tasks that require synthesis of information across multi-cellular contexts. Targeted fine-tuning with modern reinforcement learning techniques produces strong performance in perturbation response prediction, natural language interpretation, and complex biological reasoning. This predictive strength enabled a dual-context virtual screen that nominated the kinase inhibitor silmitasertib (CX-4945) as a candidate for context-selective upregulation of antigen presentation. Experimental assessment in human cell models unseen during training supported this prediction, demonstrating that C2S-Scale can effectively guide the discovery of context-conditioned biology. C2S-Scale unifies transcriptomic and textual data at unprecedented scales, surpassing both specialized single-cell models and general-purpose LLMs to provide a platform for next-generation single-cell analysis and the development of “virtual cells.”",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract presents a new LLM (C2S-Scale) trained on transcriptomic data and biological text, enabling natural language interpretation and biological reasoning, satisfying all criteria for a primary text-bio generative model. | The abstract describes a scaled LLM (27B parameters) trained on transcriptomic data and biological text, explicitly stating generative capabilities and broad task applicability, meeting criteria for a generative text-bio foundational model.","The abstract presents a new LLM (C2S-Scale) trained on transcriptomic data and biological text, enabling natural language interpretation and biological reasoning, satisfying all criteria for a primary text-bio generative model.","The abstract describes a scaled LLM (27B parameters) trained on transcriptomic data and biological text, explicitly stating generative capabilities and broad task applicability, meeting criteria for a generative text-bio foundational model.",,Scaling Large Language Models for Next-Generation Single-Cell Analysis.,"A bstract Single-cell RNA sequencing has transformed our understanding of cellular diversity, yet current single-cell foundation models (scFMs) remain limited in their scalability, flexibility across diverse tasks, and ability to natively integrate textual information. In this work, we build upon the Cell2Sentence (C2S) framework, which represents scRNA-seq profiles as textual “cell sentences,” to train Large Language Models (LLMs) on a corpus comprising over one billion tokens of transcriptomic data, biological text, and metadata. Scaling the model to 27 billion parameters yields consistent improvements in predictive and generative capabilities and supports advanced downstream tasks that require synthesis of information across multi-cellular contexts. Targeted fine-tuning with modern reinforcement learning techniques produces strong performance in perturbation response prediction, natural language interpretation, and complex biological reasoning. This predictive strength enabled a dual-context virtual screen that nominated the kinase inhibitor silmitasertib (CX-4945) as a candidate for context-selective upregulation of antigen presentation. Experimental assessment in human cell models unseen during training supported this prediction, demonstrating that C2S-Scale can effectively guide the discovery of context-conditioned biology. C2S-Scale unifies transcriptomic and textual data at unprecedented scales, surpassing both specialized single-cell models and general-purpose LLMs to provide a platform for next-generation single-cell analysis and the development of “virtual cells.”",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract introduces C2S-Scale, a new 27B‑parameter LLM trained on scRNA‑seq “cell sentences” and biological text, explicitly integrating transcriptomic and textual information, which satisfies the primary model, bio modality, text component, and text‑bio bridge criteria. | The abstract introduces C2S-Scale, a 27B‑parameter LLM trained on transcriptomic and textual data, explicitly noting predictive and generative capabilities, indicating a primary generative foundation model.","The abstract introduces C2S-Scale, a new 27B‑parameter LLM trained on scRNA‑seq “cell sentences” and biological text, explicitly integrating transcriptomic and textual information, which satisfies the primary model, bio modality, text component, and text‑bio bridge criteria.","The abstract introduces C2S-Scale, a 27B‑parameter LLM trained on transcriptomic and textual data, explicitly noting predictive and generative capabilities, indicating a primary generative foundation model.",,Scaling Large Language Models for Next-Generation Single-Cell Analysis.,"A bstract Single-cell RNA sequencing has transformed our understanding of cellular diversity, yet current single-cell foundation models (scFMs) remain limited in their scalability, flexibility across diverse tasks, and ability to natively integrate textual information. In this work, we build upon the Cell2Sentence (C2S) framework, which represents scRNA-seq profiles as textual “cell sentences,” to train Large Language Models (LLMs) on a corpus comprising over one billion tokens of transcriptomic data, biological text, and metadata. Scaling the model to 27 billion parameters yields consistent improvements in predictive and generative capabilities and supports advanced downstream tasks that require synthesis of information across multi-cellular contexts. Targeted fine-tuning with modern reinforcement learning techniques produces strong performance in perturbation response prediction, natural language interpretation, and complex biological reasoning. This predictive strength enabled a dual-context virtual screen that nominated the kinase inhibitor silmitasertib (CX-4945) as a candidate for context-selective upregulation of antigen presentation. Experimental assessment in human cell models unseen during training supported this prediction, demonstrating that C2S-Scale can effectively guide the discovery of context-conditioned biology. C2S-Scale unifies transcriptomic and textual data at unprecedented scales, surpassing both specialized single-cell models and general-purpose LLMs to provide a platform for next-generation single-cell analysis and the development of “virtual cells.”",stable_include,INCLUDE,,,primary_model_paper,yes,yes,yes,yes,,,,,"The abstract describes a new 27‑billion‑parameter LLM trained on scRNA‑seq and biological text, integrating transcriptomic and natural‑language data, which indicates a primary model with both a biological modality and a substantive text‑bio bridge. | The abstract presents a 27 B parameter LLM trained on large transcriptomic and textual corpora, explicitly noting generative capabilities, thus qualifying as a primary generative text‑bio foundation model.","The abstract describes a new 27‑billion‑parameter LLM trained on scRNA‑seq and biological text, integrating transcriptomic and natural‑language data, which indicates a primary model with both a biological modality and a substantive text‑bio bridge.","The abstract presents a 27 B parameter LLM trained on large transcriptomic and textual corpora, explicitly noting generative capabilities, thus qualifying as a primary generative text‑bio foundation model.",
