./data/docling_uncertain_biorxiv_chrome_no_vlm_2026-07-08/documents/rec_002228_2025_08_23_671699v2_full.docling.json:4814:      "orig": "A natural approach for using language models with single-cell data is to convert single-cell omics data to 'cell sentences' [8, 12], which represent each cell as a ranked list of its most highly expressed genes. We hypothesize that this approach also enables the integration of dataset-specific metadata available from increasingly complex and multi-layered datasets [13, 14, 15] and related contextual information retrieved from scientific literature. Yet, to achieve this, text- and expression-derived information needs to be combined. Here, we explore a contrastive alignment strategy to learn a joint embedding space in which expression profiles and literature-encoded knowledge are directly comparable.",
./data/docling_uncertain_biorxiv_chrome_no_vlm_2026-07-08/documents/rec_002228_2025_08_23_671699v2_full.docling.json:4815:      "text": "A natural approach for using language models with single-cell data is to convert single-cell omics data to 'cell sentences' [8, 12], which represent each cell as a ranked list of its most highly expressed genes. We hypothesize that this approach also enables the integration of dataset-specific metadata available from increasingly complex and multi-layered datasets [13, 14, 15] and related contextual information retrieved from scientific literature. Yet, to achieve this, text- and expression-derived information needs to be combined. Here, we explore a contrastive alignment strategy to learn a joint embedding space in which expression profiles and literature-encoded knowledge are directly comparable."
./data/docling_uncertain_biorxiv_chrome_no_vlm_2026-07-08/documents/rec_002228_2025_08_23_671699v2_full.docling.json:4868:      "orig": "Current research for the application of pre-trained language models for single-cell data focuses mainly on large language models (LLMs) that can be employed for diverse primary analysis tasks such as cell-type annotation, batch integration, and perturbation prediction. For example, C2S-Scale [8] uses LLMs like Pythia [18], which are additionally trained on cell sentences to support both traditional single-cell tasks and novel applications like dataset interpretation via natural language queries. Other approaches leverage general-purpose LLMs (e.g., GPT-3.5) to create cell embeddings based on expression-weighted gene vectors [9, 19], or for cluster-based annotation [7]. These approaches",
./data/docling_uncertain_biorxiv_chrome_no_vlm_2026-07-08/documents/rec_002228_2025_08_23_671699v2_full.docling.json:4869:      "text": "Current research for the application of pre-trained language models for single-cell data focuses mainly on large language models (LLMs) that can be employed for diverse primary analysis tasks such as cell-type annotation, batch integration, and perturbation prediction. For example, C2S-Scale [8] uses LLMs like Pythia [18], which are additionally trained on cell sentences to support both traditional single-cell tasks and novel applications like dataset interpretation via natural language queries. Other approaches leverage general-purpose LLMs (e.g., GPT-3.5) to create cell embeddings based on expression-weighted gene vectors [9, 19], or for cluster-based annotation [7]. These approaches"
./data/docling_uncertain_biorxiv_chrome_no_vlm_2026-07-08/documents/rec_002228_2025_08_23_671699v2_full.docling.json:5759:      "orig": "Therefore, we propose a more targeted approach for taking information from biomedical literature into account, specifically focusing on smaller task-specific language models for adding layers of information to the original single-cell data. In particular, we train small encoder-only language models [16, 17] to align gene expression profiles with biomedical literature, learning a shared semantic embedding space that integrates both data-derived and text-derived information. To build text-based datasets with a consistent structural configuration from both scRNA-seq data- and literature-derived information, we construct cell sentences and collect biomedical literature retrieved from PubMed [23] based on available metadata. Each cell sentence is paired with a similar and a dissimilar sentence to form a triplet [24, 25], and corresponding triplets are constructed from PubMed titles and abstracts. This triplet structure allows for efficient alignment of literature-derived and scRNA-seq-based cell sentences using contrastive loss functions to capture semantic relationships in the embedding [26, 27].",
./data/docling_uncertain_biorxiv_chrome_no_vlm_2026-07-08/documents/rec_002228_2025_08_23_671699v2_full.docling.json:5760:      "text": "Therefore, we propose a more targeted approach for taking information from biomedical literature into account, specifically focusing on smaller task-specific language models for adding layers of information to the original single-cell data. In particular, we train small encoder-only language models [16, 17] to align gene expression profiles with biomedical literature, learning a shared semantic embedding space that integrates both data-derived and text-derived information. To build text-based datasets with a consistent structural configuration from both scRNA-seq data- and literature-derived information, we construct cell sentences and collect biomedical literature retrieved from PubMed [23] based on available metadata. Each cell sentence is paired with a similar and a dissimilar sentence to form a triplet [24, 25], and corresponding triplets are constructed from PubMed titles and abstracts. This triplet structure allows for efficient alignment of literature-derived and scRNA-seq-based cell sentences using contrastive loss functions to capture semantic relationships in the embedding [26, 27]."
./data/docling_uncertain_biorxiv_chrome_no_vlm_2026-07-08/documents/rec_002228_2025_08_23_671699v2_full.docling.json:6841:      "orig": "Our approach builds on scRNA-seq datasets that have been processed by a conventional analysis workflow (Fig. 1 left) with annotated cell types, e.g., based on cluster-specific differential gene expression analysis [28, 29]. Starting from the gene expression count matrix(Fig. 1, Step 1), we transformed the top expressed highly-variable genes of each cell into a list of gene symbols and concatenate them to cell sentences. In addition to the gene list, metadata can be added to the cell sentence to allow for incorporating additional, dataset-specific contextual information (e.g., on donor or cell types).",
./data/docling_uncertain_biorxiv_chrome_no_vlm_2026-07-08/documents/rec_002228_2025_08_23_671699v2_full.docling.json:6842:      "text": "Our approach builds on scRNA-seq datasets that have been processed by a conventional analysis workflow (Fig. 1 left) with annotated cell types, e.g., based on cluster-specific differential gene expression analysis [28, 29]. Starting from the gene expression count matrix(Fig. 1, Step 1), we transformed the top expressed highly-variable genes of each cell into a list of gene symbols and concatenate them to cell sentences. In addition to the gene list, metadata can be added to the cell sentence to allow for incorporating additional, dataset-specific contextual information (e.g., on donor or cell types)."
./data/docling_uncertain_biorxiv_chrome_no_vlm_2026-07-08/documents/rec_002228_2025_08_23_671699v2_full.docling.json:8382:      "orig": "Figure 1: Overview of the approach for adding contextual layers of information to scRNA-seq datasets. Left: A conventional single-cell RNA-seq workflow processes a gene expression matrix through a quantitative pipeline comprising dimensionality reduction and differential expression analysis for annotation of cell-type clusters. 1) In our approach, we translate single-cell profiles into cell sentences by ranking highly expressed genes and appending metadata for generating the cell-derived dataset (A). In parallel, we build a literature-derived dataset by retrieving PubMed titles and abstracts related to the known or suspected cell types, capturing complementary information such as cell-type functionality ( function ), disease relationships ( disease ) or lineage trajectories ( time ). For both datasets, we construct label-aware triplets. 2) On the triplets, we train models with a Siamese-BERT [17] architecture, using 3) contrastive fine-tuning to align the embeddings of single cells, cell-type labels and literature-derived information. 4) The resulting joint embedding space enables context-aware analysis of scRNA-seq data with additional layers of biological information captured in the biomedical literature.",
./data/docling_uncertain_biorxiv_chrome_no_vlm_2026-07-08/documents/rec_002228_2025_08_23_671699v2_full.docling.json:8383:      "text": "Figure 1: Overview of the approach for adding contextual layers of information to scRNA-seq datasets. Left: A conventional single-cell RNA-seq workflow processes a gene expression matrix through a quantitative pipeline comprising dimensionality reduction and differential expression analysis for annotation of cell-type clusters. 1) In our approach, we translate single-cell profiles into cell sentences by ranking highly expressed genes and appending metadata for generating the cell-derived dataset (A). In parallel, we build a literature-derived dataset by retrieving PubMed titles and abstracts related to the known or suspected cell types, capturing complementary information such as cell-type functionality ( function ), disease relationships ( disease ) or lineage trajectories ( time ). For both datasets, we construct label-aware triplets. 2) On the triplets, we train models with a Siamese-BERT [17] architecture, using 3) contrastive fine-tuning to align the embeddings of single cells, cell-type labels and literature-derived information. 4) The resulting joint embedding space enables context-aware analysis of scRNA-seq data with additional layers of biological information captured in the biomedical literature."
./data/docling_uncertain_biorxiv_chrome_no_vlm_2026-07-08/documents/rec_002228_2025_08_23_671699v2_full.docling.json:9354:      "orig": "beddings of biologically related entities to cluster together while separating dissimilar ones. (Fig. 1, Step 3; Methods Section 5.7). This results in a joint embedding space where complementary textual information and cell sentences are aligned in a biological meaningful way by design (Fig. 1, Step 3). After fine-tuning, we visualized the learned embedding using UMAP [30].",
./data/docling_uncertain_biorxiv_chrome_no_vlm_2026-07-08/documents/rec_002228_2025_08_23_671699v2_full.docling.json:9355:      "text": "beddings of biologically related entities to cluster together while separating dissimilar ones. (Fig. 1, Step 3; Methods Section 5.7). This results in a joint embedding space where complementary textual information and cell sentences are aligned in a biological meaningful way by design (Fig. 1, Step 3). After fine-tuning, we visualized the learned embedding using UMAP [30]."
./data/docling_uncertain_biorxiv_chrome_no_vlm_2026-07-08/documents/rec_002228_2025_08_23_671699v2_full.docling.json:9381:      "orig": "Taken together, the approach allows for enriching representations of single-cell profiles by adding layers of complementary contextual information, such as cell type, function, disease, or time (Fig. 1, Step 4). After assessing the alignment of cell sentences and cell-type labels to confirm that embeddings reflect known cell identities, we illustrate how the learned embeddings capture richer, literatureinformed relationships, enabling annotation based on unseen functional descriptions, discovery of disease associations, and recovery of developmental trajectories when including temporal metadata.",
./data/docling_uncertain_biorxiv_chrome_no_vlm_2026-07-08/documents/rec_002228_2025_08_23_671699v2_full.docling.json:9382:      "text": "Taken together, the approach allows for enriching representations of single-cell profiles by adding layers of complementary contextual information, such as cell type, function, disease, or time (Fig. 1, Step 4). After assessing the alignment of cell sentences and cell-type labels to confirm that embeddings reflect known cell identities, we illustrate how the learned embeddings capture richer, literatureinformed relationships, enabling annotation based on unseen functional descriptions, discovery of disease associations, and recovery of developmental trajectories when including temporal metadata."
./data/docling_uncertain_biorxiv_chrome_no_vlm_2026-07-08/documents/rec_002228_2025_08_23_671699v2_full.docling.json:9436:      "orig": "With our label-aware training strategy, we aim to preserve consistency and ensure label alignment across gene-expression- and literature-derived training data. To evaluate the effectiveness of this approach, we investigated the alignment of cell sentences with their cell-type labels on a T cell-specific subset of a primary blood mononuclear cell atlas, specifically the Human Immune Health atlas from the Allen Institute (HIAI dataset) [13]. The embedding of the base language model before fine-tuning showed poor separation of cell subtypes in a UMAP representation, where embeddings of cell-type labels formed a separate cluster distant from the corresponding single-cell embeddings (Fig. 2 A 1)). Next, we trained a model on a literature-based dataset only, using the cell-type annotation in the HIAI dataset as queries. We observed improved alignment between cell-type labels and the corresponding single-cell profiles but no clear clustering by subtype (Fig. 2 A 2)). Finally, fine-tuning the model on both literature- and expression-based datasets resulted in an embedding with distinct cell clusters by subtype, with their respective cell-type label embedded within those clusters (Fig. 2 A 3)). This indicates that fine-tuning was effective for differentiating cells of different types based on their top expressed genes. Additionally, we observed meta-clusters of CD4+ and CD8+ T cells and a tendency of cells with similar functionality to also cluster together in the embedding, such as memory CD8+ T cells and \u03b3\u03b4 T cells, both characterized through strong cytotoxic capabilities [31, 32]. This is supported by assessment of marker gene expression in the model embeddings (see Additional file 1, Fig. S2), confirming that the model embeddings capture biologically meaningful marker gene expression patterns while preserving within-cell-type heterogeneity.",
./data/docling_uncertain_biorxiv_chrome_no_vlm_2026-07-08/documents/rec_002228_2025_08_23_671699v2_full.docling.json:9437:      "text": "With our label-aware training strategy, we aim to preserve consistency and ensure label alignment across gene-expression- and literature-derived training data. To evaluate the effectiveness of this approach, we investigated the alignment of cell sentences with their cell-type labels on a T cell-specific subset of a primary blood mononuclear cell atlas, specifically the Human Immune Health atlas from the Allen Institute (HIAI dataset) [13]. The embedding of the base language model before fine-tuning showed poor separation of cell subtypes in a UMAP representation, where embeddings of cell-type labels formed a separate cluster distant from the corresponding single-cell embeddings (Fig. 2 A 1)). Next, we trained a model on a literature-based dataset only, using the cell-type annotation in the HIAI dataset as queries. We observed improved alignment between cell-type labels and the corresponding single-cell profiles but no clear clustering by subtype (Fig. 2 A 2)). Finally, fine-tuning the model on both literature- and expression-based datasets resulted in an embedding with distinct cell clusters by subtype, with their respective cell-type label embedded within those clusters (Fig. 2 A 3)). This indicates that fine-tuning was effective for differentiating cells of different types based on their top expressed genes. Additionally, we observed meta-clusters of CD4+ and CD8+ T cells and a tendency of cells with similar functionality to also cluster together in the embedding, such as memory CD8+ T cells and \u03b3\u03b4 T cells, both characterized through strong cytotoxic capabilities [31, 32]. This is supported by assessment of marker gene expression in the model embeddings (see Additional file 1, Fig. S2), confirming that the model embeddings capture biologically meaningful marker gene expression patterns while preserving within-cell-type heterogeneity."
./data/docling_uncertain_biorxiv_chrome_no_vlm_2026-07-08/documents/rec_002228_2025_08_23_671699v2_full.docling.json:18456:      "orig": "To assess whether our approach can effectively incorporate both literature-derived knowledge and metadata, we included individuals' cytomegalovirus (CMV) status into the cell sentences and aligned them with CMV-related literature. Fig. 4 A shows the CMV status across all cells in the UMAP representation of the original scRNA-seq dataset. We computed similarity scores between each cell embedding and the interaction description 'Increased cytotoxic capabilities in CMV positive individuals' and employed a Mann-Whitney-U test to evaluate whether CMV-positive and CMV-negative cells could be separated within each cell type based on the cell-wise similarity scores (see Methods Section 5.10.5). As shown in Fig. 4 B , CMV-positive cells within memory CD4+ T, memory CD8+ T and \u03b3\u03b4 T cells have significantly higher similarity scores compared to CMV-negative cells. Notably, CD4+",
